{
 "rows": [
  {
   "sentence": 1,
   "start": 0.2,
   "speech_end": 7.774,
   "text": "In 1940, in a small Tennessee town, Wilma Rudolph was born, the twentieth of twenty-two children in a poor family.",
   "first_word": "In 1940",
   "end": 8.174
  },
  {
   "sentence": 2,
   "start": 8.174,
   "speech_end": 14.291,
   "text": "At four, she caught polio, her left leg grew weak, and doctors said she would never walk again.",
   "first_word": "At",
   "end": 14.691
  },
  {
   "sentence": 3,
   "start": 14.691,
   "speech_end": 22.73,
   "text": "Her mother refused to give up, taking her by bus every week to a hospital in Nashville, while her brothers and sisters rubbed her leg every day.",
   "first_word": "Her",
   "end": 23.13
  },
  {
   "sentence": 4,
   "start": 23.13,
   "speech_end": 28.548,
   "text": "For years she wore a metal brace, until at twelve, she took it off and played basketball.",
   "first_word": "For",
   "end": 28.948
  },
  {
   "sentence": 5,
   "start": 28.948,
   "speech_end": 34.481,
   "text": "She began to run, and at just sixteen, she won an Olympic bronze medal in the relay.",
   "first_word": "She",
   "end": 34.881
  },
  {
   "sentence": 6,
   "start": 34.881,
   "speech_end": 41.703,
   "text": "In 1960, in Rome, she won three gold medals, and the world called her the fastest woman on Earth.",
   "first_word": "In 1960",
   "end": 42.103
  },
  {
   "sentence": 7,
   "start": 42.103,
   "speech_end": 48.883,
   "text": "Back home, she insisted her welcome parade be open to everyone, and it became her town's first integrated event.",
   "first_word": "Back",
   "end": 49.233
  }
 ],
